# HashSet  

Deterministic hash set built on top of `HashMap<T, HashSetMarker>`.  

## Overview  

`HashSet<T>` stores only keys. Internally it delegates storage, probing,  
resizing, and drop behavior to `HashMap<T, HashSetMarker>`.  

This means the set inherits the same deterministic hashing behavior as  
`HashMap` while exposing a simpler presence/absence API.  

## Marker Payload  

`HashSetMarker` is a zero-sized marker payload used internally so the set  
can be implemented on top of `HashMap` without storing a meaningful value.  

## How It Works  

`HashSet<T>` is intentionally thin. It does not reimplement probing,  
resizing, tombstones, or raw storage management.  

Instead, it delegates to `HashMap<T, HashSetMarker>` and translates the map  
API into set semantics:  

```text  
  HashSet::insert(value)  
    -> HashMap::insert(value, HashSetMarker {})  
    -> returns true only when the key was absent  

  HashSet::contains(value)  
    -> HashMap::get(value)  
    -> presence/absence only  

  HashSet::remove(value)  
    -> HashMap::remove(value)  
    -> returns true only when the key existed  
```  

This keeps the public API simple while reusing the proven `HashMap`  
implementation for all hash-table mechanics.  

## Key Requirements  

Elements must satisfy `Hash & Eq`.  

## Example  

```ignis  
import Eq from "std::collections";  
import Hash from "std::collections";  
import HashSet from "std::collections";  
import Hasher from "std::hash";  

@implements(Hash, Eq)  
record Key {  
  id: i32;  

  hash(&self, hasher: &mut Hasher): void {  
    let mut state: &mut Hasher = hasher;  
    state.writeI32(self.id);  
  }  

  equals(&self, other: &Key): boolean {  
    return self.id == other.id;  
  }  
}  

function main(): i32 {  
  let mut set: HashSet<Key> = HashSet::new<Key>();  
  set.insert(Key { id: 1 });  

  let lookup: Key = Key { id: 1 };  
  return set.contains(&lookup) ? 0 : 1;  
}  
```


# FFI  

Types for safe Foreign Function Interface interoperability.  

All types live under the `FFI` namespace (re-exported from `std::ffi/cstring`).  

## Types  

| Type             | Source file  | Description                                       |  
|------------------|--------------|---------------------------------------------------|  
| `FFI::CString`   | cstring.ign  | Owned, NUL-terminated C string with RAII cleanup   |  
| `FFI::NulError`  | cstring.ign  | Error: input contained interior NUL bytes          |  

## CString vs String vs str  

| Type           | Ownership | NUL-terminated | Interior NULs  | Use Case           |  
|----------------|-----------|----------------|----------------|--------------------|  
| `str`          | Borrowed  | Yes (contract) | Allowed        | String literals    |  
| `String`       | Owned     | Yes (runtime)  | Allowed        | General-purpose    |  
| `FFI::CString` | Owned     | Yes (guaranteed)| **Prohibited** | FFI, C interop     |  

## Safety  

`FFI::CString` guarantees:  
- No interior NUL bytes (checked at construction)  
- Always NUL-terminated  
- Owned buffer freed automatically on drop  

## Example  

```ignis  
import FFI from "std::ffi";  
import LibC from "std::libc";  

function main(): i32 {  
  let result: Result<FFI::CString, FFI::NulError> = FFI::CString::new("/tmp/file.txt");  

  if (result.isOk()) {  
    let cstr: FFI::CString = result.unwrap();  
    let ptr: LibC::CType::CConstStr = cstr.asStr();  
    // Pass ptr to C function expecting const char*  
    return 0;  
  }  

  return 1;  
}  
```


# FFI CString  

Owned, NUL-terminated C-compatible string for FFI interop, exposed as  
`FFI::CString` and `FFI::NulError`.  

## Types  

| Type       | Description                                              |  
|------------|----------------------------------------------------------|  
| `CString`  | Owned NUL-terminated buffer with RAII cleanup (Drop)     |  
| `NulError` | Error: input contained interior NUL bytes at `position`  |  

## Construction  

| Method                          | Source          | Cost                    |  
|---------------------------------|-----------------|-------------------------|  
| `FFI::CString::new(s)`          | `str` literal   | `memchr` + `memcpy`     |  
| `FFI::CString::ofString(&s)`    | `&String`       | `memchr` + `memcpy`     |  
| `FFI::CString::fromRawOwned(p)` | Raw `*mut u8`   | `strlen` (no copy)      |  

## Example  

```ignis  
import FFI from "std::ffi";  
import LibC from "std::libc";  

function main(): i32 {  
  let result: Result<FFI::CString, FFI::NulError> = FFI::CString::new("hello");  
  if (result.isOk()) {  
    let cstr: FFI::CString = result.unwrap();  
    let ptr: LibC::CType::CConstStr = cstr.asStr();  
    return 0;  
  }  
  return 1;  
}  
```


# Format Module  

Formatting traits for user-defined textual representations.  

## Exports  

| Export    | Description                                               |  
|-----------|-----------------------------------------------------------|  
| `Display` | `display(&self): String`, with a reflective record default and implementations for primitives, `str`, `String`, `Option` and `Vector` |  

## Example  

```ignis  
@implements(Display)  
record Point {  
  public x: i32;  
  public label: String;  
}  

let point: Point = Point { x: 1, label: String::create("origin") };  
let text: String = point.display(); // Point { x: 1, label: origin }  
```  

Enums still need a hand-written `display`: the default walks record  
fields only (see `std::format::display`).


# Filesystem  

Filesystem operations with structured error handling.  

All fallible operations return `Result<T, Io::IoError>` instead of  
raw errno codes. Import `Io` alongside `Fs` to inspect errors.  

## Module Tree  

```  
std::fs                     (this file — hub, re-exports Fs namespace)  
  std::fs::sys::mod         (platform dispatch layer)  
    std::fs::sys::unix      (POSIX syscall wrappers — Linux/glibc)  
```  

## Sub-namespaces  

| Namespace       | Description                                |  
|-----------------|--------------------------------------------|  
| `Fs::Sys`       | Platform syscall wrappers (selected at compile time) |  

## Types  

| Type                    | Description                                         |  
|-------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|  
| `Fs::Sys::Stat`         | File metadata from `stat(2)` / `fstat(2)`           |  
| `Fs::Sys::SysDirEntry`  | Single directory entry from `readdir(3)`            |  

## Constants  

Open flags, seek modes, permission bits, and dirent type constants  
are available under `Fs::Sys`. See `sys/unix.ign` for the full list.  

## Platform  

Currently Linux and macOS (POSIX). The `Fs::Sys` layer is designed so  
future targets can add sibling backend modules and the dispatch hub  
(`sys/mod.ign`) selects at compile time via `@configFlag`.  

## Example  

```ignis  
import Fs from "std::fs";  
import Io from "std::io";  

function main(): i32 {  
  // stat a file  
  let result: Result<Fs::Sys::Stat, Io::IoError> =  
    Fs::Sys::stat("/etc/hosts");  

  if result.isOk() {  
    let st: Fs::Sys::Stat = result.unwrap();  
    // st.size, st.mode, st.uid, st.gid, ...  
    return 0;  
  }  
  return 1;  
}  
```  

```ignis  
import Fs from "std::fs";  
import Io from "std::io";  

function main(): i32 {  
  // write a file  
  let fd: Result<i32, Io::IoError> = Fs::Sys::open(  
    "/tmp/hello.txt",  
    Fs::Sys::O_WRONLY | Fs::Sys::O_CREAT | Fs::Sys::O_TRUNC,  
    438,  
  );  
  if fd.isError() { return 1; }  

  let rawFd: i32 = fd.unwrap();  
  let msg: *u8 = "hello\n";  
  Fs::Sys::write(rawFd, msg as *mut void, 6);  
  Fs::Sys::close(rawFd);  
  return 0;  
}  
```


# Directory Iteration  

Owned iterator over directory entries with automatic cleanup.  

`Fs::ReadDir` is an owned directory iterator that wraps `DIR*` and  
automatically calls `closedir` when dropped. Each call to `next()`  
returns an owned `DirEntry` with copied strings.  

## Example  

```ignis  
import Fs from "std::fs";  
import Io from "std::io";  

function main(): i32 {  
  let result: Result<Fs::ReadDir, Io::IoError> = Fs::ReadDir::open("/tmp");  
  if (result.isError()) {  
    return 1;  
  }  

  let mut iter: Fs::ReadDir = result.unwrap();  

  loop {  
    match (iter.next()) {  
      Result::OK(Option::SOME(entry)) -> {  
        if (entry.isDir()) {  
          Io::println(entry.name);  
        }  
      },  
      Result::OK(Option::NONE) -> {  
        break;  
      },  
      Result::ERROR(_) -> {  
        return 1;  
      },  
    }  
  }  

  return 0;  
}  
```


# File Type  

RAII wrapper around a POSIX file descriptor.  

`Fs::File` owns a file descriptor and automatically closes it when dropped.  
Use this instead of raw `open`/`close` calls for automatic resource cleanup.  

## Construction  

| Method | Description |  
|--------|-------------|  
| `File::open(path)` | Open existing file for reading |  
| `File::create(path)` | Create/truncate file for writing |  
| `File::openWithFlags(path, flags, mode)` | Full control over open flags |  

## Example  

```ignis  
import Fs from "std::fs";  
import Io from "std::io";  

function main(): i32 {  
  let result: Result<Fs::File, Io::IoError> = Fs::File::open("/etc/hosts");  
  if (result.isError()) {  
    return 1;  
  }  

  let mut file: Fs::File = result.unwrap();  

  let meta: Result<Fs::Metadata, Io::IoError> = file.metadata();  

  return 0;  
}  
```


# File Metadata  

Platform-independent file metadata and file type classification.  

`Fs::Metadata` wraps the raw POSIX `Stat` record and exposes a  
portable API. `Fs::FileType` is an enum that classifies entries  
as regular files, directories, or symlinks without requiring  
callers to know about `S_IFMT` masks or `DT_*` constants.  

## Types  

| Type       | Description                                          |  
|------------|------------------------------------------------------|  
| `Metadata` | File size, timestamps, permissions, and file type    |  
| `FileType` | Classification: `FILE`, `DIRECTORY`, `SYMLINK`, `OTHER` |  

## Construction  

Users do not create `Metadata` directly. Instead, obtain it from:  
- `Fs::metadata(path)` (stat by path)  
- `file.metadata()` (fstat on open file)  

## Example  

```ignis  
import Fs from "std::fs";  
import Io from "std::io";  

function main(): i32 {  
  let result: Result<Fs::Metadata, Io::IoError> = Fs::metadata("/etc/hosts");  
  if (result.isError()) {  
    return 1;  
  }  

  let meta: Fs::Metadata = result.unwrap();  
  if (meta.isFile()) {  
    let size: i64 = meta.len();  
    return 0;  
  }  

  return 1;  
}  
```


# I/O Streams  

Buffered byte reading from a file descriptor, whole-buffer writes, and  
`Content-Length` message framing, exposed under the `Stream` namespace and  
re-exported by `std::io`.  

## Types and Functions  

| Item                               | Description                                        |  
|------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|  
| `Stream::ByteReader`                   | Buffered reader over a file descriptor             |  
| `Stream::ByteReader::stdin`            | Reader over standard input                         |  
| `Stream::ByteReader::readExact`        | Exactly `count` bytes                              |  
| `Stream::ByteReader::readLine`         | One line terminated by `\r\n`                      |  
| `Stream::ByteReader::readFramedMessage`| One `Content-Length` framed message body           |  
| `Stream::ByteReader::readFramedMessageWithin`| Same, skipping bodies over a byte limit      |  
| `Stream::ByteReader::skipExact`        | Discard exactly `count` bytes                      |  
| `Stream::ReadError`                    | End of input, truncated input, or a failed read    |  
| `Stream::FrameError`                   | Why a framed message could not be read             |  
| `Stream::ByteWriter`                   | Unbuffered whole-buffer writer over a descriptor   |  
| `Stream::writeAll`                     | Write a whole `String` to a file descriptor        |  
| `Stream::writeFramedMessage`           | Write one framed message to stdout                 |  
| `Stream::writeFramedMessageTo`         | Write one framed message to a file descriptor      |  

## Framing  

A framed message is a block of `Name: value` header lines, each ended by  
`\r\n`, then an empty line, then exactly `Content-Length` bytes of body:  

```text  
  Content-Length: 17\r\n  
  Content-Type: application/vscode-jsonrpc; charset=utf-8\r\n  
  \r\n  
  {"jsonrpc":"2.0"}  
```  

`Content-Length` is required, its name matches case-insensitively, and its  
value is a decimal byte count with optional surrounding spaces or tabs.  
Every other header, `Content-Type` included, is ignored.  

## Reads and Writes  

`read(2)` and `write(2)` may transfer fewer bytes than requested and may be  
interrupted by a signal. `ByteReader` keeps reading until it has the bytes  
it needs or reaches end of input, and `writeAll` keeps writing until every  
byte is written. Both retry on `EINTR`. There is no user-space write  
buffer, so the bytes have reached the descriptor when `writeAll` returns.  

A `ByteReader` does not own its descriptor: the caller closes it. The  
reader may read ahead of the data it returns, so do not mix it with other  
reads of the same descriptor.  

## Example  

```ignis  
import Stream from "std::io";  

function main(): i32 {  
  let mut reader: Stream::ByteReader = Stream::ByteReader::stdin();  

  while (true) {  
    let body: String = match (reader.readFramedMessage()) {  
      Result::OK(body) -> body,  
      Result::ERROR(Stream::FrameError::END_OF_INPUT) -> {  
        return 0;  
      },  
      Result::ERROR(_) -> {  
        return 1;  
      },  
    };  

    let Result::OK(_) = Stream::writeFramedMessage(&body) else {  
      return 1;  
    };  
  }  

  return 0;  
}  
```


# LibC  

Raw bindings to a POSIX-oriented subset of the C standard library.  

All types use the `CType` namespace (re-exported from `std::libc/primitives`).  
No safety guarantees are added -- the caller is responsible for pointer  
validity, buffer sizes, and lifetime management.  

## Sub-namespaces  

| Namespace              | Source file  | C Headers                | Description                                    |  
|------------------------|--------------|--------------------------|------------------------------------------------|  
| `LibC::Allocator`      | memory.ign   | `stdlib.h`               | `malloc`, `calloc`, `realloc`, `free`          |  
| `LibC::MemoryOperations` | memory.ign | `string.h`               | `memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset`, `memcmp`        |  
| `LibC::Memory`         | memory.ign   | `sys/mman.h`, `unistd.h` | `mmap`, `munmap`, `mprotect`, `sbrk`, `brk`    |  
| `LibC::String`         | string.ign   | `string.h`               | `strlen`, `strcmp`, `strcpy`, `strcat`, ...     |  
| `LibC::File`           | io.ign       | `unistd.h`, `fcntl.h`    | `open`, `close`, `read`, `write`, `lseek`, ... |  
| `LibC::Stdio`          | stdio.ign    | `stdio.h`                | `fopen`, `fclose`, `fread`, `fwrite`, ...      |  
| `LibC::Errno`          | errno.ign    | `errno.h`                | `get`, `set` + 26 POSIX error constants        |  
| `LibC::Process`        | process.ign  | `stdlib.h`, `unistd.h`   | `exit`, `fork`, `getpid`, `sleep`, ...         |  
| `LibC::Signals`        | process.ign  | `signal.h`               | `raise`, `kill`                                |  
| `LibC::Wait`           | process.ign  | `sys/wait.h`             | `wait`, `waitpid`                              |  
| `LibC::Conversion`     | misc.ign     | `stdlib.h`               | `atoi`, `atol`, `atof`                         |  
| `LibC::Math`           | misc.ign     | `stdlib.h`               | `abs`, `labs`                                  |  
| `LibC::Character`      | misc.ign     | `ctype.h`                | `isalnum`, `isdigit`, `toupper`, `tolower`, ...|  
| `LibC::Time`           | misc.ign     | `time.h`                 | `time`, `clock`, `clock_gettime`, clock IDs    |  

## Constants  

Each sub-namespace re-exports the C constants it needs from  
`std::libc::platform`. On `x86_64` Linux their values are written in Ignis  
and checked against the C headers by `scripts/check_platform_constants.py`;  
other targets read the header macro of the same name.  

| Namespace          | Constants                                                        |  
|--------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|  
| `LibC::File`       | `SEEK_SET`, `SEEK_CUR`, `SEEK_END`, `STDIN_FILENO`, `STDOUT_FILENO`, `STDERR_FILENO`, `O_RDONLY`, `O_WRONLY`, `O_RDWR`, `O_CREAT`, `O_TRUNC`, `O_APPEND` |  
| `LibC::Stdio`      | `SEEK_SET`, `SEEK_CUR`, `SEEK_END`, `EOF`                        |  
| `LibC::Errno`      | `EPERM`, `ENOENT`, `EACCES`, `EINVAL`, `ENOMEM`, `EAGAIN`, ...  |  
| `LibC::Process`    | `EXIT_SUCCESS`, `EXIT_FAILURE`                                   |  
| `LibC::Signals`    | `SIGINT`, `SIGKILL`, `SIGSEGV`, `SIGTERM`                        |  
| `LibC::Memory`     | `PROT_NONE`, `PROT_READ`, `PROT_WRITE`, `PROT_EXEC`, `MAP_SHARED`, `MAP_PRIVATE`, `MAP_ANONYMOUS` |  
| `LibC::Time`       | `CLOCKS_PER_SEC`, `CLOCK_REALTIME`, `CLOCK_MONOTONIC`            |  

## Platform  

Type sizes assume LP64 (Linux/macOS 64-bit). See `CType` for the  
full type mapping.  

## Example  

```ignis  
import LibC, CType from "std::libc";  

function main(): i32 {  
  // Write to stdout via file descriptor  
  let msg: *u8 = "hello from libc\n";  
  LibC::File::write(LibC::File::STDOUT_FILENO, msg as CType::CConstVoidPtr, 16);  

  // Open a file, write to it, close it  
  let fd: CType::CInt = LibC::File::open("out.txt", LibC::File::O_WRONLY | LibC::File::O_CREAT | LibC::File::O_TRUNC);  
  if fd != -1 {  
    let data: *u8 = "written via libc\n";  
    LibC::File::write(fd, data as CType::CConstVoidPtr, 17);  
    LibC::File::close(fd);  
  }  

  // Check errno after a failed operation  
  let bad: CType::CInt = LibC::File::open("/nonexistent", LibC::File::O_RDONLY);  
  if bad == -1 {  
    let err: CType::CInt = LibC::Errno::get();  
    if err == LibC::Errno::ENOENT {  
      LibC::Process::exit(LibC::Process::EXIT_SUCCESS);  
    }  
  }  

  return 0;  
}  
```


# LibC Memory  

Memory allocation, byte-level operations, and virtual memory management.  

## Sub-namespaces  

| Namespace                | C Header              | Description                                |  
|--------------------------|-----------------------|--------------------------------------------|  
| `LibC::Allocator`        | `stdlib.h`            | `malloc`, `calloc`, `realloc`, `free`      |  
| `LibC::MemoryOperations` | `string.h`            | `memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset`, `memcmp`    |  
| `LibC::Memory`           | `sys/mman.h`, `unistd.h` | `mmap`, `munmap`, `mprotect`, `sbrk`, `brk` |  

## Constants (`LibC::Memory`)  

| Constant         | Description                          |  
|------------------|--------------------------------------|  
| `PROT_NONE`      | No access permitted                  |  
| `PROT_READ`      | Pages may be read                    |  
| `PROT_WRITE`     | Pages may be written                 |  
| `PROT_EXEC`      | Pages may be executed                |  
| `MAP_SHARED`     | Share mapping across processes       |  
| `MAP_PRIVATE`    | Private copy-on-write mapping        |  
| `MAP_ANONYMOUS`  | Mapping not backed by a file         |  

## Example  

```ignis  
import LibC, CType from "std::libc";  

function main(): i32 {  
  // Heap allocation  
  let buf: CType::CVoidPtr = LibC::Allocator::malloc(256);  
  LibC::MemoryOperations::memset(buf, 0, 256);  
  LibC::Allocator::free(buf);  

  // Anonymous mmap  
  let page: CType::CVoidPtr = LibC::Memory::mmap(  
    null, 4096,  
    LibC::Memory::PROT_READ | LibC::Memory::PROT_WRITE,  
    LibC::Memory::MAP_PRIVATE | LibC::Memory::MAP_ANONYMOUS,  
    -1, 0,  
  );  
  LibC::Memory::munmap(page, 4096);  

  return 0;  
}  
```


# LibC Miscellaneous  

Conversion, integer math, character classification, and time.  

## Sub-namespaces  

| Namespace            | C Header    | Description                            |  
|----------------------|-------------|----------------------------------------|  
| `LibC::Conversion`   | `stdlib.h`  | `atoi`, `atol`, `atof`                 |  
| `LibC::Math`         | `stdlib.h`  | `abs`, `labs`                          |  
| `LibC::Character`    | `ctype.h`   | `isalnum`, `isdigit`, `toupper`, ...   |  
| `LibC::Time`         | `time.h`    | `time`, `clock`, `CLOCKS_PER_SEC`      |  

## Example  

```ignis  
import LibC, CType from "std::libc";  

function main(): i32 {  
  // String-to-number conversion  
  let n: CType::CInt = LibC::Conversion::atoi("42");  

  // Character classification  
  let ch: CType::CInt = 65; // 'A'  
  let lower: CType::CInt = LibC::Character::tolower(ch);  

  // CPU time measurement  
  let start: CType::ClockT = LibC::Time::clock();  
  // ... work ...  
  let elapsed: CType::ClockT = LibC::Time::clock() - start;  

  return 0;  
}  
```


# LibC String  

Null-terminated C string operations from `string.h`, exposed as  
`LibC::String`.  

## Functions  

| Function    | Description                                      |  
|-------------|--------------------------------------------------|  
| `strlen`    | Length of null-terminated string                  |  
| `strcmp`     | Compare two strings (returns 0 if equal)         |  
| `strncmp`   | Compare up to `n` bytes                          |  
| `strcpy`    | Copy string including null terminator             |  
| `strncpy`   | Copy up to `n` bytes, null-padding if shorter     |  
| `strcat`    | Append string to end of destination               |  
| `strncat`   | Append up to `n` bytes                           |  
| `strchr`    | Find first occurrence of byte in string           |  
| `strrchr`   | Find last occurrence of byte in string            |  
| `strstr`    | Find first occurrence of substring                |  
| `strdup`    | Duplicate string (caller must `free`)             |  
| `strerror`  | Human-readable string for an errno value          |  

All functions operate on raw `CType::CStr` / `CType::CConstStr` pointers.  
The caller is responsible for buffer sizes and null termination.  
Do not use these APIs for Ignis `String` byte-length semantics; they are  
raw C-string helpers and may stop at the first interior NUL byte.  

## Example  

```ignis  
import LibC, CType from "std::libc";  

function main(): i32 {  
  let msg: CType::CConstStr = "hello, world";  
  let len: CType::SizeT = LibC::String::strlen(msg);  

  // Duplicate and compare  
  let copy: CType::CStr = LibC::String::strdup(msg);  
  let eq: CType::CInt = LibC::String::strcmp(msg, copy);  
  LibC::Allocator::free(copy as CType::CVoidPtr);  

  // Find substring  
  let pos: CType::CStr = LibC::String::strstr(msg, "world");  

  return 0;  
}  
```


# Math Module  

Double-precision math wrappers backed by the C math library (`libm`).  

## Function Reference  

| Category       | Functions                                         |  
|----------------|---------------------------------------------------|  
| Constants      | `PI`, `E`, `TAU`                                  |  
| Trigonometry   | `sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `asin`, `acos`, `atan`, `atan2` |  
| Exponential    | `exp`, `log`, `log10`, `pow`, `sqrt`              |  
| Rounding       | `floor`, `ceil`, `round`, `trunc`                 |  
| Absolute value | `fabs`                                            |  

All functions operate on `f64` and follow the underlying libm semantics  
of the target platform.  

## Example  

```ignis  
import Math from "std::math";  
import Io from "std::io";  

function main(): i32 {  
  let angle: f64 = Math::PI / 2.0;  
  let value: f64 = Math::sin(angle);  
  let dist: f64 = Math::sqrt((3.0 * 3.0) + (4.0 * 4.0));  

  Io::println(String::toString(value));  
  Io::println(String::toString(dist));  
  return 0;  
}  
```


# Pointer Module  

Thin wrapper for raw pointers with small utility methods.  

## Overview  

`Pointer<T>` does not add ownership or safety guarantees.  
It only groups common pointer operations into a single record API.  

## What This Type Is  

`Pointer<T>` is a convenience wrapper around `*mut T` that provides:  
- Null checks  
- Address conversion helpers  
- Pointer arithmetic helpers  
- Read/write helpers for direct memory access  

It is useful when low-level code wants method-call style APIs while still  
operating on raw pointers.  

## What This Type Is Not  

It is not a smart pointer and does not:  
- Manage memory lifetime  
- Prevent aliasing bugs  
- Validate bounds  
- Guarantee alignment  

## Safety  

The caller is responsible for ensuring all pointer operations are valid.  
Dereferencing a null, dangling, unaligned, or out-of-bounds pointer is  
undefined behavior.  

## Example  

```ignis  
import Pointer from "std::ptr";  
import Memory from "std::memory";  
import Io from "std::io";  

function main(): i32 {  
  let raw: *mut i32 = Memory::allocateVector<i32>(2);  
  let mut ptr: Pointer<i32> = Pointer::init(raw);  

  ptr.write(10);  
  ptr.writeAt(1, 20);  

  Io::println(String::toString(ptr.read()));  
  Io::println(String::toString(ptr.readAt(1)));  

  Memory::free(raw);  
  return 0;  
}  
```


# Thread pool  

A fixed number of worker threads that run queued jobs.  

## Exports  

| Export       | Description                                        |  
|--------------|----------------------------------------------------|  
| `ThreadPool` | `new(size)` starts the workers; `execute` queues a job |  

## Running jobs  

`ThreadPool::new(size)` starts `size` workers and panics when `size` is 0.  
`execute(task)` queues `task` and returns at once; the first idle worker  
takes it. Jobs start in the order they were queued, but with more than one  
worker they run concurrently and may finish in any order. A job that wants  
to report back sends on a `std::sync::Channel`.  

The task parameter is `@sendable`, the same as `Thread::spawn`'s, so the  
compiler checks that whatever the closure captures can move to a worker.  

## Dropping the pool  

Dropping the pool queues one exit signal per worker behind the jobs already  
queued, and joins every worker before it returns. The queue is first in,  
first out, so the workers take every earlier job before any of them exits:  
every job queued before the drop runs to completion first. A job that  
never returns keeps the drop waiting forever.  

## Panics  

A panic inside a job ends the whole process with exit code 101, as any  
panic in a thread does.  

## Ownership of a job  

A closure is neither Send nor Sync, so a job cannot travel through a  
`Channel<() -> void>`. `execute` boxes the closure in its own heap cell, the  
way `Thread::spawn` does, and sends the cell as a `PoolJob`. A worker moves  
the closure out of the cell, frees it and runs the closure; a job that is  
never run drops the closure and frees the cell.  

## Example  

```ignis  
import Channel, Receiver, Sender, ThreadPool from "std::sync";  

function main(): i32 {  
  let channel: Channel<i32> = Channel::new<i32>();  
  let sender: Sender<i32> = channel.sender;  
  let receiver: Receiver<i32> = channel.receiver;  

  {  
    let pool: ThreadPool = ThreadPool::new(2);  

    pool.execute((): void -> {  
      (@move sender).send(42);  
    });  
  }  

  return receiver.recv().unwrapOr(0);  
}  
```


# Shared  

`Arc<Mutex<T>>` under a shorter name.  

Sharing one mutable value between threads means an `Arc` around a `Mutex`  
at every site that passes it, so the standard library declares the pair  
once. The alias is transparent: `Shared<Store>` is `Arc<Mutex<Store>>`  
wherever it is written, in a local annotation, a type argument or the  
receiver of a method call, and the bounds of `Arc` and `Mutex` still apply  
to `Store`.  

Spell `Arc<Mutex<T>>` out instead when the surrounding code is  
lock- and guard-heavy enough that naming which half owns the count and  
which half owns the value reads clearer.  

## Example  

```ignis  
import Arc, Mutex, Shared from "std::sync";  

function main(): i32 {  
  let counter: Shared<u64> = Arc::new<Mutex<u64>>(Mutex::new<u64>(0));  
  let other: Shared<u64> = counter.clone();  

  return *other.get().lock().read() as i32;  
}  
```


# Test Assertions  

Native testing helpers for `@test` functions.  

The `std::test` module provides the standard assertion and snapshot surface  
used by `ignis test`.  

It is intentionally small and stable:  
- `Test::assert(condition)`  
- `Test::assertEq<T>(left, right)`  
- `Test::assertNe<T>(left, right)`  
- `Test::fail()`  
- `Test::assertSnapshot(name, actual)`  
- `Test::assertFileSnapshot(name, filePath)`  

## Design Goals  

- keep language-level tests deterministic  
- make failures explicit and easy to spot in runner output  
- route generic equality through the canonical `std::hash::Eq` contract  
- store snapshots next to the source module under test  
- avoid compiler-only test APIs leaking into user code  

## Assertions  

`assert`, `assertEq`, and `assertNe` all fail the current test by calling  
`@panic(...)` with a fixed message.  

`assertEq<T>` and `assertNe<T>` rely on builtin `@eq<T>`. Supported `T`  
includes:  

- integer primitives  
- `boolean`  
- `char`  
- `str`  
- records and enums implementing canonical `std::hash::Eq`  

Unsupported equality must be rejected during analysis before code generation  
or harness build.  

## Snapshots  

Snapshot helpers compare UTF-8 text against deterministic files under a  
sibling `__snapshots__/` directory.  

File naming is derived from:  

- the fully-qualified test name  
- the user-supplied snapshot label  

Both parts are escaped so snapshots from different modules can safely reuse  
the same logical label.  

Update behavior is driven by the native runner through environment variables:  

- `IGNIS_TEST_NAME`  
- `IGNIS_TEST_SNAPSHOT_DIR`  
- `IGNIS_TEST_UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS`  

With update mode enabled, missing or mismatched snapshots are written back to  
disk. Without update mode, the helpers fail the current test.  

## Failure Output  

Snapshot mismatches print small, bounded diagnostics to stderr before  
panicking:  

- failure reason  
- snapshot path  
- expected byte count  
- actual byte count  

The native runner is responsible for truncating long stderr/stdout blocks in  
the final report.  

## Example  

```ignis  
import Test from "std::test";  

@test  
function smoke(): void {  
  Test::assert(true);  
  Test::assertEq<i32>(2 + 2, 4);  
}  
```  

## Generic Equality Example  

```ignis  
import Eq from "std::hash";  
import Test from "std::test";  

@implements(Eq)  
record UserId {  
  public value: i32;  

  equals(&self, other: &UserId): boolean {  
    return self.value == other.value;  
  }  
}  

@test  
function compareUserIds(): void {  
  let left: UserId = UserId { value: 7 };  
  let right: UserId = UserId { value: 7 };  
  let other: UserId = UserId { value: 8 };  

  Test::assertEq<UserId>(left, right);  
  Test::assertNe<UserId>(left, other);  
}  
```  

## Snapshot Example  

```ignis  
import Test from "std::test";  

@test  
function snapshotRenderedOutput(): void {  
  Test::assertSnapshot("rendered", "hello snapshot\n");  
}  
```  

## File Snapshot Example  

```ignis  
import Test from "std::test";  

@test  
function snapshotGeneratedFile(): void {  
  Test::assertFileSnapshot("artifact", "./actual-output.txt");  
}  
```


# Web  

A minimally opinionated web framework in the spirit of Express, built on  
`std::http`. Middleware and routes take the request, the response and the  
next function, and return `Result<void, Web::Error>`; an error that  
reaches the router becomes a response with its status.  

## Exports  

| Export              | Description                                                         |  
|---------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|  
| `Web::Router`       | `new()`, `use`, `get`, `post`, `put`, `patch`, `delete`, `any`, `route`, `dispatch`; implements `Http::Handler` |  
| `Web::App`          | Trait: `build(&self): Web::Router`, called once per server thread   |  
| `Web::listen`       | Parses `"127.0.0.1:8080"` and serves an app with the default configuration |  
| `Web::listenOn`     | Serves an app from a host and a port                                |  
| `Web::listenWith`   | Serves an app from an address and a `Web::Config`                   |  
| `Web::parseAddress` | Parses `"<host>:<port>"`; `":8080"` and `"*:8080"` are every interface |  
| `Web::Config`       | `threads`; `new()`, `toServerConfig()`                              |  
| `Web::ListenError`  | A failed start; `message()` renders what was expected or the bind failure |  
| `Web::serve`        | The layer below: binds a `SocketAddress` and serves an app on `ServerConfig.threads` threads |  
| `Web::serveListener` | The layer below: serves an app on a listener already bound         |  
| `Web::Middleware`   | `(&mut Request, &mut Response, Next) -> Result<void, Error>`        |  
| `Web::Next`         | `(&mut Request, &mut Response) -> Result<void, Error>`              |  
| `Web::Request`      | `http`; `create(&http)`, `method()`, `target()`, `path()`, `header(name)`, `body()`, `bodyText()`, `param(name)`, `query(name)` |  
| `Web::Response`     | `status`, `headers`, `body`; `new()`, `setStatus`, `header`, `setHeader`, `addHeader`, `write`, `writeStr`, `text`, `json`, `finish` |  
| `Web::Error`        | `status`, `message`; `new(message)` (500), `withStatus(status, message)` |  
| `Web::Param`        | One route parameter: `name` and decoded `value`                     |  

## Starting a server  

`Web::listen` parses an address string, binds it and serves the app:  
`"127.0.0.1:8080"` binds that interface, and `":8080"` or `"*:8080"`  
binds every interface, `0.0.0.0`. `Web::listenOn` takes a host and a  
port, `Web::listenWith` a `Web::Config` (the thread count), and  
`Web::parseAddress` parses without serving. A malformed address is a  
`Web::ListenError` whose message names what was expected and what was  
given; names are not resolved. `Web::serve` and `Web::serveListener`  
are the layer below: they take the `std::net::SocketAddress` and the  
`std::http::ServerConfig` directly.  

## Middleware and routes  

Both have the `Web::Middleware` shape. A middleware added with `use`  
runs for every request, in the order it was added, before the routes.  
Calling `next` runs the rest of the chain and returns once it finished,  
so code after `next` runs on the way back out (the onion model), and an  
error from `next` propagates with `!` like any other. `next` takes the  
request and the response back as arguments: a closure holding them would  
keep them borrowed for as long as it lives.  

A middleware that does not call `next` ends the chain; returning an  
error does too. A route that calls `next` passes the request to the next  
route that matches it; when there is none, the answer is 404.  

## Routing  

A route matches by method and path. `get` also answers `HEAD` (the  
server sends the head without the body); `any` answers every method;  
`route` takes any method name as written on the request line.  

The path is compared segment by segment, before any `?` and without  
decoding. A segment `:name` matches any non-empty segment and makes its  
percent-decoded value available as `request.param("name")`; any other  
segment matches only itself. Every segment counts, so a trailing slash  
is significant: `/users/:id` matches `/users/7` but not `/users/7/`, and  
`/users/` does not match `/users`. A parameter that is not valid  
percent-encoded UTF-8 is answered with 400.  

When no route matches, the answer is 404. When some route matches the  
path but none its method, the answer is 405 with an `Allow` header  
listing the methods that would match. Both run through the middleware  
as errors, so a logger sees them.  

`request.query("name")` decodes the first query parameter with that  
name: `+` is a space, `%XX` an escaped byte, and a parameter written  
without `=` has an empty value. A value that is not valid  
percent-encoded UTF-8 is `NONE`.  

## Errors  

`Web::Error` carries a status (500 by default) and a message. The router  
answers an error with that status, `Content-Type: text/plain;  
charset=utf-8` and the message as the body, keeping header fields that  
were already set. Client input never makes the router panic.  

## Threads  

Function values are neither `Send` nor `Sync`, so a router cannot be  
shared between threads. An application is a record implementing  
`Web::App`; `Web::serve` shares it among `ServerConfig.threads` threads,  
each of which calls `build` once and serves its connections with its own  
router through `Http::acceptConnections`. State shared by every thread  
lives in the app record and has to be `Send` and `Sync`, behind an `Arc`,  
a `Mutex` or an atomic.  

A router is also an `Http::Handler`, so `Http::serveConnection` serves  
one connection with it, which is how a test drives it.  

## Example  

```ignis  
import Io from "std::io";  
import Web from "std::web";  

function logger(  
  request: &mut Web::Request,  
  response: &mut Web::Response,  
  next: Web::Next  
): Result<void, Web::Error> {  
  let result: Result<void, Web::Error> = next(request, response);  
  Io::println(`${request.method().toString()} ${request.path()} ${response.status}`);  
  return result;  
}  

function showUser(  
  request: &mut Web::Request,  
  response: &mut Web::Response,  
  next: Web::Next  
): Result<void, Web::Error> {  
  let Option::SOME(id) = request.param("id") else {  
    return Result::ERROR(Web::Error::withStatus(400, "missing id"));  
  };  

  let text: String = String::create(id);  

  if (text.equals("0")) {  
    return Result::ERROR(Web::Error::withStatus(404, "no such user"));  
  }  

  response.text(`user ${id}`);  
  return Result::OK(());  
}  

@implements(Web::App)  
record Users {  
  public greeting: str;  

  build(&self): Web::Router {  
    let greeting: str = self.greeting;  
    let mut router: Web::Router = Web::Router::new();  
    router.use(logger);  
    router.get(  
      "/",  
      (request: &mut Web::Request, response: &mut Web::Response, next: Web::Next): Result<void, Web::Error> -> {  
        response.text(greeting);  
        return Result::OK(());  
      },  
    );  
    router.get("/users/:id", showUser);  
    return router;  
  }  
}  

function main(): i32 {  
  let app: Users = Users { greeting: "hello" };  

  return Web::listen<Users>("127.0.0.1:8080", app).isOk() ? 0 : 1;  
}  
```

items.doc:
Returns whether `value` is an alphabetic Unicode scalar.  

This covers the scalar ranges needed by current parser/diagnostic text  
handling without pretending that `char` literals are raw bytes.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Char from "std::char";  

let latin: char = 'é';  
let cjk: char = '中';  
let digit: char = '５';  

latin.isAlpha(); // true  
cjk.isAlpha();   // true  
digit.isAlpha(); // false  
```

Returns whether `value` is an alphabetic or numeric Unicode scalar.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Char from "std::char";  

let letter: char = 'A';  
let digit: char = '５';  
let underscore: char = '_';  

letter.isAlphanumeric();     // true  
digit.isAlphanumeric();      // true  
underscore.isAlphanumeric(); // false  
```

Returns whether `value` is an ASCII alphabetic character.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Char from "std::char";  

let ascii: char = 'A';  
let unicode: char = 'é';  

ascii.isAsciiAlpha();    // true  
unicode.isAsciiAlpha();  // false  
```

Returns whether `value` is ASCII alphabetic or numeric.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Char from "std::char";  

let letter: char = 'x';  
let underscore: char = '_';  

letter.isAsciiAlphanumeric();     // true  
underscore.isAsciiAlphanumeric(); // false  
```

Returns whether `value` is an ASCII hexadecimal digit.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Char from "std::char";  

let hex: char = 'f';  
let other: char = 'g';  

hex.isAsciiHexDigit();   // true  
other.isAsciiHexDigit(); // false  
```

Returns whether `value` is an ASCII lowercase letter.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Char from "std::char";  

let lower: char = 'a';  
let upper: char = 'A';  

lower.isAsciiLowercase(); // true  
upper.isAsciiLowercase(); // false  
```

Returns whether `value` is an ASCII decimal digit.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Char from "std::char";  

let ascii: char = '7';  
let fullWidth: char = '５';  

ascii.isAsciiNumeric();     // true  
fullWidth.isAsciiNumeric(); // false  
```

Returns whether `value` is an ASCII uppercase letter.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Char from "std::char";  

let upper: char = 'A';  
let lower: char = 'a';  

upper.isAsciiUppercase(); // true  
lower.isAsciiUppercase(); // false  
```

Returns whether `value` is ASCII whitespace supported by Ignis escapes.  

This includes space, line feed, carriage return, and tab.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Char from "std::char";  

let newline: char = '\n';  
let letter: char = 'A';  

newline.isAsciiWhitespace(); // true  
letter.isAsciiWhitespace();  // false  
```

Returns whether `value` is a numeric Unicode scalar.  

This includes ASCII digits, Arabic-Indic digits, and full-width digits.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Char from "std::char";  

let ascii: char = '7';  
let fullWidth: char = '５';  

ascii.isNumeric();     // true  
fullWidth.isNumeric(); // true  
```

Returns whether `value` is a Unicode whitespace scalar.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Char from "std::char";  

let space: char = ' ';  
let nonBreakingSpace: char = 160 as char;  

space.isWhitespace();            // true  
nonBreakingSpace.isWhitespace(); // true  
```

Converts an ASCII uppercase letter to lowercase.  

Non-uppercase characters are returned unchanged.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Char from "std::char";  

let upper: char = 'A';  
let unicode: char = 'é';  

upper.toAsciiLowercase();   // 'a'  
unicode.toAsciiLowercase(); // 'é'  
```

Converts an ASCII lowercase letter to uppercase.  

Non-lowercase characters are returned unchanged.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Char from "std::char";  

let lower: char = 'a';  
let unicode: char = 'é';  

lower.toAsciiUppercase();   // 'A'  
unicode.toAsciiUppercase(); // 'é'  
```

members.doc:
Creates an empty map with no allocated buckets.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashMap from "std::collections";  

let map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new<i32, i32>();  
```

Drops all occupied entries but keeps the bucket allocation for reuse.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashMap from "std::collections";  

let mut map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new<i32, i32>();  
map.insert(1, 10);  
map.clear();  
```

Returns `true` if `key` is present in the map.  

This probes directly without materializing an intermediate `Option<&V>`.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashMap from "std::collections";  

let mut map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new<i32, i32>();  
map.insert(1, 10);  
let found: boolean = map.contains(&1);  
```

Returns an immutable reference to the value for `key`, if present.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashMap from "std::collections";  

let mut map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new<i32, i32>();  
map.insert(7, 99);  
let value: Option<&i32> = map.get(&7);  
```

Returns a mutable reference to the value for `key`, if present.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashMap from "std::collections";  

let mut map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new<i32, i32>();  
map.insert(7, 99);  
let value: Option<&mut i32> = map.getMut(&7);  
```

Removes `key` from the map and returns its previous value, if present.  

Removal leaves a tombstone bucket behind so probe chains remain valid.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashMap from "std::collections";  

let mut map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new<i32, i32>();  
map.insert(1, 10);  
let removed: Option<i32> = map.remove(&1);  
```

Ensures enough buckets exist to insert `additional` more entries without  
violating the current load-factor policy.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashMap from "std::collections";  

let mut map: HashMap<i32, i32> = HashMap::new<i32, i32>();  
map.reserve(32);  
```

Creates an empty set with no allocated buckets.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashSet from "std::collections";  

let set: HashSet<i32> = HashSet::new<i32>();  
```

Removes all values while preserving the current bucket allocation.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashSet from "std::collections";  

let mut set: HashSet<i32> = HashSet::new<i32>();  
set.insert(1);  
set.clear();  
```

Returns `true` if `value` is already present.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashSet from "std::collections";  

let mut set: HashSet<i32> = HashSet::new<i32>();  
set.insert(7);  
let found: boolean = set.contains(&7);  
```

Removes `value` from the set.  

Returns `true` when the value existed and `false` otherwise.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashSet from "std::collections";  

let mut set: HashSet<i32> = HashSet::new<i32>();  
set.insert(7);  
let removed: boolean = set.remove(&7);  
```

Ensures the set can accommodate `additional` more elements without  
immediate growth.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import HashSet from "std::collections";  

let mut set: HashSet<i32> = HashSet::new<i32>();  
set.reserve(32);  
```

items.doc:
Returns an owned copy of the environment variable when it is present.  

The returned string is copied from host-owned storage before it leaves this  
function. A present but empty variable returns `Option::SOME("")`; only an  
absent variable returns `Option::NONE`.  

# Arguments  

- `name`: environment variable name to query.  

# Returns  

- `Option::SOME(value)` when the variable exists. `value` is an owned copy.  
- `Option::NONE` when the variable is absent.  

# Invariants  

- Empty variables are still present: `NAME=` returns `Option::SOME("")`.  
- The returned `String` does not borrow from the host environment.  
- Later `Env::set` or `Env::unset` calls do not invalidate earlier results.  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Env from "std::env";  

Env::set("IGNIS_MODE", "debug");  
match (Env::get("IGNIS_MODE")) {  
  Option::SOME(value) -> {  
    // value is an owned String containing "debug".  
  },  
  Option::NONE -> {},  
};  
```

members.doc:
Whether the connection stays open after this request (RFC 9112  
section 9.3): HTTP/1.1 keeps it unless `Connection` lists `close`;  
HTTP/1.0 closes it unless `Connection` lists `keep-alive`.

items.doc:
Prints `message` to stderr without a trailing newline.  

# Arguments  

* `message` - The String to print (consumed/moved).  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Io from "std::io";  

Io::eprint(String::create("warning: "));  
Io::eprintln(String::create("low disk space"));  
```

Prints `message` to stdout followed by a newline (`\n`).  

This is the most common way to produce output. The String is  
moved into the function and dropped after printing.  

# Arguments  

* `message` - The String to print (consumed/moved).  

# Example  

```ignis  
import Io from "std::io";  

Io::println(String::create("hello"));  
Io::println("hello from str");  
Io::println(String::create(42));  
```

